#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069571} :activity/id #uuid "acac3ae8-22e4-541d-b0da-b38b7e642d06" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050657 :topic/id #uuid "5cf444d7-d014-59c7-b47c-21b442662b58" :topic/math-academy-id 474} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069571 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069959 :tutorial/content "A function $f(x)$ is said to be **left-continuous** at the point $x=c$ if the left-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to c^-} f(x) = f(c).\n$$\n\nTo demonstrate, let's consider the function $y=f(x)$ defined on the interval $(-\\infty,2]$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/14/14b92b0e5a0f482b5e58da975f6f8d369890af97ddc562a4876e41c496f5dfef.png)\n\nNotice that at $x=2,$ the left limit exists, while right limit does not exist because $f(x)$ is not defined for $x>2.$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 2^-}f(x) = 4, \\qquad \\lim_{x\\to 2^+}f(x) = \\text{DNE}\n$$\n\nBecause the right-sided limit does not exist, the overall limit does not exist either:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 2}f(x) = \\text{DNE}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=2.$ However, because the left-sided limit exists and is equal to the function value at $x=2,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 2^-}f(x) = 4 = f(2),\n$$\n\nwe can say that the function is **left-continuous** at $x=2.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "75d31b95-b022-5133-8b0d-54a5ba38c74f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2596 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "27206838-7dfd-50f0-a4b5-a92905b05017" :step/math-academy-id 12117 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069573}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069572 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068698 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068550 :question/id #uuid "0acc185c-cc26-523f-981a-3e024f0693a6" :question/math-academy-id "e-3589"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff9486fe-3ee1-5917-88de-97251c889552" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049593} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050454}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069371} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069372} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233082} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233083} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234090} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315085} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315086} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315108} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329879} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341902}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Function is Left- or Right-Continuous From a Graph"} :step/id #uuid "b43b6d85-ec3f-5921-878b-59ca9e5c066f" :step/math-academy-id 12118 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069574}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069573 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069960 :tutorial/content "A function $f(x)$ is said to be **right-continuous** at the point $x=c$ if the right-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to c^+} f(x) = f(c).\n$$\n\nTo demonstrate, let's consider the function $y=f(x)$ defined on the interval $[-2,\\infty)$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/0b/0b625f271c8889466c2a19ed8a333b05e70c2e852058cc1d4469f428d761c799.png)\n\nThe function is **right-continuous** at $x=-2$ because\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -2^+}f(x) = 4 = f(-2).\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "9424b0a9-43f3-5c35-b8b9-b78b9c69e816" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2597 :tutorial/title "Right Continuity"} :step/id #uuid "55c60e5f-cbe7-5688-8af0-b8fa5d2c54d1" :step/math-academy-id 12119 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069572}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069574 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068699 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068551 :question/id #uuid "e9a8cbce-3bed-567b-9e0e-e1382465a681" :question/math-academy-id "e-3590"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2dc967c7-840e-5717-98f2-330fcf0fe12d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049226}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069311} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069315} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233893} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234333} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240840} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240841} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240842} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314797} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314799} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314800} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341907}] :knowledge-point/title "Using the Definition of Left- and Right-Continuity to Compute a Limit"} :step/id #uuid "b88e81b2-e4ca-5ffc-8ade-1a44b0fecea1" :step/math-academy-id 12120 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069577}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069575 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068700 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068552 :question/id #uuid "1ec6e675-6052-5cec-8b29-766084245f74" :question/math-academy-id "e-3591"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "81247553-750f-5bf7-a1c4-698ad63b5ae4" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069312} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069313} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234080} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234335} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234336} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240855} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240856} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326681} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326691} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341908} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341909}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Left- and Right-Continuity"} :step/id #uuid "1017d5f3-a1be-5979-aa84-1860d0b66f77" :step/math-academy-id 12121} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069577 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069962 :tutorial/content "Recall that a function $f(x)$ is said to be **continuous** at a point $x=c$ if \n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x \\to c} f(x) = f(c) .\n$$\n\nIn order for the above statement to be true, we require both\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {c}^{-}}f(x)=f(c)\\,\\text{and}\\,{lim}_{x\\to {c}^{+}}f(x)=f(c).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equalities represent left-continuity and right-continuity. So, we have the following fact:\n\n> A function is **continuous** at a point if and only if it is *both* **left-continuous** *and* **right-continuous** at that point." :tutorial/id #uuid "1dd8ea3c-49f9-5c85-98b3-85b0542e7a8f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2641 :tutorial/title "Defining Continuity in Terms of Left-Continuity and Right-Continuity"} :step/id #uuid "c70c04f3-5555-555a-93b3-8518806961e9" :step/math-academy-id 12456 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069575}}] :activity/title "Left and Right Continuity" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068998}]}
